module

import Leancrypto.Codec.Hex
import Leancrypto.Rsa

/-!
Verification vectors, both kinds.

The `A` and `B` cases are from the NIST CAVP file `SigVer15_186-3.rsp` (in
`186-3rsatestvectors.zip`, the FIPS 186-3 RSA vectors), 2048-bit modulus with SHA-256; that file
records each case as `Result = P` or `Result = F`, and the names below say which. The `Short` case
is the first passing 1024-bit case from the same file, included because this verifier must refuse
it for its size rather than agree with it.

The last three blocks appear in no published suite, because building them needs a private key: a
signature whose recovered block is right apart from one padding byte, and one whose padding is cut
to the eight bytes the standard requires with the `DigestInfo` shifted down and the space that
frees filled with bytes a lenient parser would step over. The second is the Bleichenbacher
forgery, and a verifier that reads the recovered block rather than comparing it accepts it. Both
were made with a throwaway key generated by
`openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048` and signed with
`openssl rsautl -raw -sign`; the genuine signature beside them is from
`openssl dgst -sha256 -sign`, and is there so that a failure of the other two is known to be the
padding rather than the fixture.

The negatives matter more than the positives. Every forgery in this family passes the positives.
-/

namespace Tests.RsaVectors
open Leancrypto

private def bytesOfHex (hex : String) : ByteArray := (Leancrypto.Codec.Hex.decodeString hex).getD ⟨#[]⟩

private def natOfHex (hex : String) : Nat := Rsa.natOfBytes (bytesOfHex hex)

private def keyOfHex (modulus exponent : String) : Rsa.PublicKey :=
  { modulus := natOfHex modulus, exponent := natOfHex exponent }

private def verify (key : Rsa.PublicKey) (message : ByteArray) (signature : String) : Bool :=
  Rsa.verifyPkcs1v15 .sha256 key message (bytesOfHex signature)

